Z||000022||ThatRickGuy||02-19-2001||04:59 AM||||I'd love to see the Fiero come back again, but I'm not holding my breath. The F-Bodies are going though, that's been getting talked about for years. What I would really like to see is the Rageous to go into production. I love my Fiero, but 3 people is a pain in the ass and you can't haul anything. vis the Rageous which has the hatch back and tail gate and suicide door, plus the vette's engine, and a kick ass set up. New Fiero = good, Production Rageous = Awesome.||||reg||1
Z||000023||rogergarrison||02-19-2001||09:15 AM|||| <P>Next, unless they were to make the car a bit longer, i don't see the north south engine mount being benificial.  It would end up putting some of the weight behind the axle, which would make it a rear mounted engine, not mid.  I've heard that rear engine cars handle like crap.<P>PORSCHE !!!!!!!!! the best and fastest Porsches have alway had the entire engine behind the rear axle. Ever notice how in sports car racing, probabley 1/2 the field are 911's<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||firstfiero||02-19-2001||09:35 AM||||    Porsche only has rear mounted engines because until they made the boxster no porsche sold very well if the engine wasn't in the rear. They have wanted to go to a mid engine car since the mid 60s but to porsche buyers if the engine isn't in the rear it isn't a porsche...I know  I had a 924. ||||reg||1
Z||000025||mrfixit58||02-19-2001||09:48 AM||||DJ --  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Bring back the Fiero to compete in the mid-enigine sports car market? After seeing the Aztek as the "Official Pace Car" of the Daytona 500, I could be convinced to believe anything.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>And, if you would have told me Big "E" would be dead after the 2001 race...<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||4
Z||000026||gtjeff||02-25-2001||02:18 AM||||if a new fiero had a ls1 it would be faster or equal to the Z06, an 88gt with a/c weighs 275 pounds LESS than chevy's supposed lightweight new vette. Car and Driver did a story on the new mosler mt 900, mid-engine car with ls1, 0-60 was listed as 3.5 seconds, Z06 is 4.0.( mosler weighs close to fiero). gm wouldnt let anything in-house outperform the vette,but vette horsepower is rumored to increase, so this idea could be feasible then.<BR>  <BR>i noticed that gm stated on their media web site a while back that speedster wont be imported here. even if they decided to sell it here, a recent article said the plant could only add 3,500 units. that wouldnt meet the demand for the car. also noticed gm has shown the mid-engine tandem concept with its fiero dash and suspension parts. the car was shown again sept 2000 at a gm tech rally in toronto.<P>They seem to be returning to the strategy of late 80's when most divisions had their own unique sports car. soon cadillac will have evoq, buick i would bet will build bengal. that leaves pontiac? . <P>i believe pontiac will have a new sports car especially in light of no news about a future firebird. lets hope its fiero. ||||reg||1
Z||000027||JohnnyK||02-25-2001||02:38 AM||||FIrst of all.. This is not my opinion, I am TELLING you guys this..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They will not bring back the Fiero. Firstly, the reputation is finished. No one would buy it, except a few die hard Fiero fans, everyone else would just make fun of it and/or call it a flaming death trap. Secondly, they already SOLD the rights to the name Fiero to suzuki.... I'm done now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000028||v8vinnie||02-25-2001||08:34 AM||||its pretty funny when i hear a smart remark about the flaming fiero death trap when i am at a car show or a cruise in, the fact is i can pick out just about every so called muscle car and tellyou their problems also, basically thats all they are , just old  cars i know i own old muscle cars, they have a certin carizma about them , but most of them are junk, i hate to say that, but they are and im an american car buff, i only buy american cars and only ride jap bikes, and one more thing , ialso had owned a 97 c5 corvette and i must admit that no one on yhe planet stock from the factory can build a better sports car that can do everything the corvette can right down to its great gas mileage for 40,000 dollars, its the best sports car dollar for dollar on the planet, just my 2-cents. but i still like my v8 87gt alot more!!!||||reg||1
Z||000029||Conrad Sanville||02-25-2001||08:44 AM||||I can hear the smug journalists of the mainstream car magazines now if the Fiero nameplate is ever re-introduced.  I remember how they all had great praise for the cars at introduction only to trash them later on.  The same thing happened to the Olds Toronado when it first came out in the fall of '65. Both the Toro and Fiero were two of GM's most radical cars that were ahead of their time.  I think they served as guinea pigs for new technology while using a unique re-arrangement of existing drivetrains.<BR>I hope they don't re-use the names on a contempory car. Just look at that hideous Monte Carlo.  ||||reg||1
Z||000030||p4n1c||02-25-2001||11:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Conrad Sanville:<BR><B>The same thing happened to the Olds Toronado when it first came out...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah first mass produced American car with front wheel drive in 1966 and first car with driver side airbag in 1974.  I think the Toro was on of the best Olds cars next to the 4-4-2.<P>The Toro was used again from 1987 to 1990 I believe, not in the same sense of what happened to the Monte Carlo when it took over the Lumina name plate, it was just redesigned like the Cutlass was.  And that car did sort of stand up to the name, 170hp V6, very sweet looking notchback.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Raydar||02-25-2001||01:33 PM||||I agree that if it happens, GM probably won't use the Fiero name. I'm guessing "Pegasus". <BR>How's that for a shot in the dark?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000032||Songman||02-25-2001||01:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by firstfiero:<BR><B>    Porsche only has rear mounted engines because until they made the boxster no porsche sold very well if the engine wasn't in the rear. They have wanted to go to a mid engine car since the mid 60s but to porsche buyers if the engine isn't in the rear it isn't a porsche...I know  I had a 924. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ever heard of the 928? NO, it wasn't as popular as the 911 but how many cars have gone for 40 years without many changes in the body. <P>The V-8 front engine 928s were very popular cars. Still would take my 911 over any 3 928s though.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000033||jlhuber||02-25-2001||06:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>FIrst of all.. This is not my opinion, I am TELLING you guys this..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They will not bring back the Fiero. Firstly, the reputation is finished. No one would buy it, except a few die hard Fiero fans, everyone else would just make fun of it and/or call it a flaming death trap. Secondly, they already SOLD the rights to the name Fiero to suzuki.... I'm done now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OK, here is one for ya.  What if Saturn produces a 2 seat, mid engine sports car that does NOT have the Fiero badge?  "Most" people don't know that Saturn is in anyway what so ever affiliated with GM/Pontiac.  If it is not made by GM, doesn't carry the Fiero badge, doesn't look exactly like the "old" Fiero, and is not advertised as being related to the "old" Fiero, how would those "most people" know that the "new Fiero" car has anything to do with the "old" Fiero?  Other than the fact that they both have two seats and are mid engine.<P>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">   <BR>If you expect the unexpected, was it really even unexpected in the first place?||||reg||1
Z||000034||JohnnyK||02-25-2001||08:30 PM||||Because GM owns Saturn?||||reg||1
Z||000035||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-26-2001||04:01 AM||||As far as I can tell, the Miata is killing the MR2 spider in terms of sales.  <P>I don't think GM will choose mid engine layouts because people like to bring lots of stuff with them.  <P>The styling fad now seems to be sports-pickup trucks like the scheduled-for-production Chevy SSR that are half supercar and half "family truckster."  I have been looking at the AutoWeeks with dismay as the 40s and 50s hot rod styling is taking over.  The key to all of this is the PT cruiser, I think.  I bet they are good cars, but I like the look of them less than the Aztek.<P>We have to face it, the Fiero was a fluke.  Pontaic cannot style a vehlicle these days without tons of little "disco" trim stips everywhere.  The cars look like a washboard from any angle.  Please god, do not let them do that to the fiero!  Can you imagine the look of it?  Todays Pontiac styling on the beautiful cars we love so much?<P>I am not bitter.  I have a Fiero and I love it.  But, the new wave of rugged sports vehicles that are just around the corner (Pontiac Vibe GT, Mistubishi RPM 7000, Lexus IS300 sportcross, etc). <P>The point is that the sport utility and sports car will be merged to form a car that can go anywhere fast.  Give me the ability to hit speed bumps at 80 MPH, do 0 to 60 in less than 7 seconds, get better than 25 MPG, and still pull 0.85g at the skid pad in a car with mobile internet access and a DVD player and I will gladly save my Fiero for Sunday drives.<P>I like to abuse cars, but I hate driving my Ford Ranger truck (built to be unaffected by many forms of neglegent driving).  My '84 SC Fiero is not too bad with bumps, but bad roads do get anyoying after a half hour or so.  Wouldn't you like to drive a car that can out handle a Mustang GT while transporting 4x8' sheets of plywood?  <P>What if you could trade stock at the same time?  People today care more about connectivity (i.e. palm computers with wirelss internet) than they do about driving.  The average person regards driving as a chore.  Don't laugh, because some day it will be impossible to buy a new car with a mechanical connection between the steering control and front wheels.  <P>The best Fieros were the ones that were built between 1984 and 1988.  History will always be clear on this point.  They were the best looking cars Pontiac ever made.  They were also the only mass-produced American mid-engine sports car.  <P>Now WE have them, and I say that the best way to express your love for these amazing cars is to keep yours running until there are no more parts to be found.  Buy parts cars and strip them, do not let anything Fiero fall into a car-crusher.  The more we treasure our cars the more people will become aware of their value.  <P>Until then, be glad that in most cases your favorite car can be had for less than $5K.  That's thousands less than a new Hyundai!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000036||ThatRickGuy||02-26-2001||10:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>The point is that the sport utility and sports car will be merged to form a car that can go anywhere fast. Give me the ability to hit speed bumps at 80 MPH, do 0 to 60 in less than 7 seconds, get better than 25 MPG, and still pull 0.85g at the skid pad in a car with mobile internet access and a DVD player and I will gladly save my Fiero for Sunday drives.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Exactly why Pontiac should produce the Rageous. with SUVs being such a big seller, and everyone wanting the hauling and 4x4 ability, with hot performance and handling, it just makes scence. A LT-1 (I beleive)drive train on some great suspension, a killer body, and enough cargo space to haul your lumber! or heck, even a bunch of runts from a soccer game. I saw the car back in Milwaukee '97, and I'm still hoping they do it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Tigger||02-26-2001||11:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>Oldsmobile and moderate don't belong in the same sentence.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Anyone know about the joint venture with Olds and Shelby?  Not sure how that's going now, I'm sure it's dead.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Just recently I seen a picture of a firebird GM specialty vehicle.  It was all the same as the current firebirds except in a wagon configuration.  It's not a new concept.  I know the idea has been thrown around inside GM for the past 25 years.  The Rageous concept is probably the most recent example.<P>I guess now that it looks like "wagons" are the new direction, GM might as well jump on that band wagon.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000038||jlhuber||02-26-2001||11:58 AM||||I think the Rageous looks cool, I even have a Hotwheels one.  Metallic blue with the black top.  If it is built like the concept, I would think about buying one.<P>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">   <BR>If you expect the unexpected, was it really even unexpected in the first place?||||reg||1
Z||000039||p4n1c||02-26-2001||07:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tigger:<BR><B> Anyone know about the joint venture with Olds and Shelby?  Not sure how that's going now, I'm sure it's dead.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hadn't heard of it, but I'm pretty sure it's dead now.  Olds was at the Toronto car show showing off their new year model line.  I sat in an Aurora, nice friggin' car.  Only had the 3.5 V6 in it though.  2002 Bravada is nice, redesigned with a 270hp inline 6.<P>Have you ever seen the Oldsmobile OSVs?  They used to have it in the Tomorrow section on their webpage, it's not there anymore I don't think.  240hp supercharged Alero, Intrigue and Silouette.  19" wheels, awesome ground effects, they looked almost like BMWs but better.  They had upgraded everything, sports suspension, sport brakes(Brembo, I think).  The Silouette looked awesome for a minivan and it was supercharged too.  To bad they didn't release those.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)||||reg||1
Z||000040||Tigger||02-26-2001||07:45 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>I sat in an Aurora, nice friggin' car.</B><P>Thanks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><B>Have you ever seen the Oldsmobile OSVs</B><P>Yes, 2000 Detroit Auto Show<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Here's the site for the <A HREF="http://www.shelbyamerican.com/series1/overview.html" TARGET=_blank>Shelby Series 1</A><P>It's powered by a specially-modified Aurora V8.  Overall, it's very similar configuration to the Vette.  All aluminum frame and high performance suspension.  Looks like only 500 will be made.<P><A HREF="http://www.shelbyamerican.com/series1/images/Gallery/car3.jpg" TARGET=_blank>See Picture</A><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 02-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Pontiaddict||02-27-2001||12:20 AM||||First, the Rageous. Does anyone see that the Chevy version is the Nomad concept. Extra doors and all. Platform sharing, hmmmmmm. As far as the looks, both versions are ugly.<P>I still think the Firebird will come back. Jim Wangers thinks so, and if anyone would know, it would be Pontiacs biggest fan.<P>The Shelby Series 1 project almost died. If another company wouldn't have bought it out, it wouldn't be around. Even now it cost twice as much as it was supposed to. Before that, there was talk about going to Ford power.<P>jlhuber is right about people not knowing that Saturn is a GM car.<P>LuckyTheyWereEverMade, I like the side strakes on Pontiacs cars, especially the Grand Am. If it wasn't FWD I'd actually want one. A Ranger pickup may be built to handle neglegant driving, but driving one nomally will kill it rather quickly.<P>As for the people who want to surf the internet and think driving is a chore, let them take their laptop onto the bus.<P>I do like my Fiero, but I hardly think that it was the best looking car ever made. The front fascia on my GT makes it look like a guppy.<P>If the MR2 is doing as bad as you say it is, then it would be more likely that GM would build a 2 seat mid engined version of it, that is if they think that one will sell. The plant(s) would be able to meet the demand, and the Vibe proves that GM is willing to sell rebadged Toyotas.<P><I>Now wouldn't that be nice</I><p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 03-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000042||jlhuber||02-27-2001||11:41 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>.... A Ranger pickup may be built to handle neglegant driving, but driving one nomally will kill it rather quickly.  ....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have one statement to make about this.  "The life of ANY vehicle depends on the mechanic"  If you do not take care of it properly, ANY car is going to die quicker than normal.  For those who say Fords do not last, HA!  My dad has a 1986 F250 with the 6.9L diesel which has over 300,000 miles on the original engine and tranny (read: never rebuilt or replaced).  All the work that the truck needed (what little there was) was done by my dad, my brother, and me.  The biggest part of this truck that ever need to be rebuilt was the fuel injector.  This truck still runs as if it were new but he does not drive it because in '98 he bought a 1999 F250 Super Duty with the 7.3L turbo diesel.  He bought it because he got tired of the "old truck" and wanted to buy a new one, he paid cash for the thing too.  Damn I love the sound of that turbo.  Sounds like the Cummins turbo in the "big-rig" tractors.  Oh, and yes, I do proudly own a Ford, a 1999 Ford Ranger 4WD to be specific.<P>I am not trying to be a dick about this, just don't judge the quality of a vehicle by its name.  If you buy a used vehicle and it starts having problems, blame the mechanic/driver not the company that built it.  With the obvious exception of manufacturing/material defects and such.<P>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">     <BR>If you expect the unexpected, was it really even unexpected in the first place?<p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||1
